# Fugue No. 6 in D minor, BWV 851 (J. S. Bach, WTC I)
#
# Character: grave and searching, three voices in 3/4. The subject
# climbs in eighths from D (D E F G E), turns in sixteenths and falls
# a sixth with a trill (F, D C# D, B-flat, G). Bach combines it with its
# own inversion: the first half is mostly the subject upright, the
# second mostly inverted, and the last bars set both against each
# other. It should be sustained and thoughtful, not driven.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 54. 44 bars of 3/4 is 132 quarters. Per-fugue track
# times (MusicBrainz track lists): Angela Hewitt (Hyperion, 1998) 2:34,
# Sviatoslav Richter 2:35, Rosalyn Tureck (2002 issue) 2:30, all about
# 51 to 53 to the quarter. Glenn Gould (1:44) is far quicker, about 76,
# and was not followed. 54 is a touch above the slow group,
# since the chip's notes fade and a long held tie would die away.
#
# Structure (Mutopia score, checked with a pattern search on the MIDI;
# every entry starts on the second eighth of its bar):
#   1-8    Exposition: subject top (1), answer on A in the middle voice
#          (3; it is the lowest voice until the bass arrives), subject
#          in the bass (6).
#   9-12   Episode: the subject's falling-sixth figure in quarter notes,
#          in canon between top and middle, over a sixteenth bass.
#   13-16  Subject on A in the top (13) against the inversion in the
#          middle voice (14): the first stretto of upright and inverted.
#   17     Cadence in A minor. Subject in the bass (17), answered a bar
#          later in the middle voice (18): stretto at one bar.
#   21     Cadence in A minor. Subject in the bass (21); inversion in
#          the top (22) and in the bass (23).
#   25     Cadence in D minor; a rising chromatic link.
#   27-29  Three-voice stretto: inversion top (27), subject middle (28),
#          inversion bass (29).
#   30-33  Episode: a falling sequence over a stepping bass, to a held
#          E in the bass (33).
#   34-35  Subject in the bass (in thirds with the middle voice),
#          turning toward G minor.
#   35-38  Episode: a rising sequence.
#   39     Cadence in D minor. Final stretto: subject in the bass (39),
#          middle voice (40).
#   43-44  Tonic pedal and the final chord with a major third (F-sharp),
#          the Picardy third.
# The exact keys of the cadences at 17 and 21 are read from the bass
# (E to A); treat them as approximate.

tempo 54

dyn 1 mp           # bars 1-12: exposition and episode, quiet and grave
dyn 13 mf          # bars 13-20: upright and inverted subject together
dyn 21 mp          # bars 21-24: after the A minor cadence the second
                   # half begins, the inversion takes over
dyn 25 mf          # bars 25-29: after the D minor cadence, the chromatic
                   # rise and the three-voice stretto
dyn 30 mp          # bars 30-33: episode, a step back
ramp 34-38 f       # bars 34-38: bass entry and the rising sequence build
                   # into the D minor cadence
                   # bars 39-44: final stretto and close at f

lead 1-2 top       # subject, top
lead 3-4 bottom    # answer in the middle voice, lowest until bar 6
lead 6-7 bottom    # subject, bass
lead 13-14 top     # subject on A, top
lead 14-15 mid     # inversion, middle voice
lead 17-18 bottom  # subject, bass
lead 18-19 mid     # subject, middle voice, a bar behind the bass
lead 21-22 bottom  # subject, bass
lead 22-23 top     # inversion, top
lead 23-24 bottom  # inversion, bass
lead 27-28 top     # inversion, top
lead 28-29 mid     # subject, middle voice
lead 29-30 bottom  # inversion, bass
lead 34-35 bottom  # subject, bass
lead 39-40 bottom  # final stretto: subject, bass
lead 40-41 mid     # final stretto: subject, middle voice

rit 3 0.75         # the last three bars: cadence and the D major chord
